result_text:
I reviewed the two changed files (`integration-tests/extensions-install.test.ts` and `integration-tests/test-helper.ts`) against all 8 angles. None of the CLAUDE.md files that apply here have rules that touch this code (the only one is `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`, which covers RuntimePulse and CodeGraph), so the conventions angle found nothing. One problem I found in unchanged code is not in the list because nothing in the diff touches it: `EOL` is used at `test-helper.ts:256` and `:575` but never imported.

```json
[
  {
    "file": "integration-tests/extensions-install.test.ts",
    "line": 28,
    "summary": "The test installs, updates and uninstalls into the developer's real ~/.gemini/extensions (ExtensionStorage.getUserExtensionsDir uses os.homedir()) and turns the extension on in user-scope settings, without isolating HOME.",
    "failure_scenario": "A developer who already has their own extension called \"test-extension\" loses it to the uninstall at the start. Other test files run in parallel (fileParallelism: true) and load user extensions from the same HOME, so they see this extension appear and vanish mid-run. Two runs on one host (e.g. sandbox none and docker) hit 'Extension \"test-extension\" is already installed'. globalSetup backs up and restores the user's memory file to avoid exactly this kind of home-directory pollution; this test does nothing similar."
  },
  {
    "file": "integration-tests/extensions-install.test.ts",
    "line": 38,
    "summary": "Uninstall and cleanup are not in a try/finally or afterEach, so any failed assertion or rejected runCommand leaves the extension installed and turned on.",
    "failure_scenario": "If the `expect(updateResult).toContain('0.0.2')` check fails, the uninstall, rig.cleanup() and rmSync never run. ~/.gemini/extensions/test-extension stays installed and turned on in the user's settings, so every later `gemini` session on that machine loads it."
  },
  {
    "file": "integration-tests/extensions-install.test.ts",
    "line": 52,
    "summary": "The unconditional rmSync(rig.testDir) runs after rig.cleanup() and ignores KEEP_OUTPUT, which rig.cleanup() deliberately respects.",
    "failure_scenario": "`npm run test:e2e` sets KEEP_OUTPUT=true so test output is kept for debugging. rig.cleanup() skips the delete, then rmSync removes the directory anyway, so the artifacts for this test are always gone. When KEEP_OUTPUT is not set, rmSync just repeats cleanup()'s `rm -rf`."
  },
  {
    "file": "integration-tests/test-helper.ts",
    "line": 323,
    "summary": "runCommand only closes the child's stdin when options.stdin is given; without it, stdin stays open.",
    "failure_scenario": "Any command that prompts or reads stdin, with no stdin option passed (e.g. an update that asks for consent like install does, via promptForContinuation/readline), blocks forever. The test then hangs until the 5-minute testTimeout and retries twice, instead of failing fast. run() closes stdin by default for this reason."
  },
  {
    "file": "integration-tests/test-helper.ts",
    "line": 342,
    "summary": "runCommand adds no 'error' listener to the spawned child, and on a non-zero exit it rejects with stderr only, dropping stdout.",
    "failure_scenario": "If the spawn itself fails (e.g. ENOENT for node, or the bundle is missing), an unhandled 'error' event is emitted and the close-based promise may never settle as intended. When a command fails and reports the reason on stdout, the rejection message has none of it, which makes CI failures hard to diagnose."
  },
  {
    "file": "integration-tests/extensions-install.test.ts",
    "line": 29,
    "summary": "The pre-test uninstall is wrapped in an empty catch that swallows every error, not just 'not installed'.",
    "failure_scenario": "If the uninstall fails for a real reason (bundle not built, a permissions error on ~/.gemini, a corrupt extension dir), the error is hidden. The install then fails with a confusing 'already installed' message, or the test runs against stale state."
  },
  {
    "file": "integration-tests/extensions-install.test.ts",
    "line": 34,
    "summary": "The assertions are weak: runCommand appends stderr to the result, and 'test-extension' / '0.0.2' can match text that is not the success message.",
    "failure_scenario": "handleUpdate prints errors with console.error and still exits 0, so an error message that echoes the name or version on stderr still ends up in the returned string. The install/list checks for 'test-extension' would pass on any output that mentions the name, e.g. the source-path echo. The test should assert the exact success text, such as 'successfully updated: 0.0.1 → 0.0.2' and 'installed successfully'."
  },
  {
    "file": "integration-tests/extensions-install.test.ts",
    "line": 35,
    "summary": "The test installs with --path pointing at the rig's whole test directory, so the extension copy includes .gemini/settings.json and telemetry.log.",
    "failure_scenario": "copyExtension copies the workspace's .gemini/settings.json (with its telemetry and sandbox settings) and the growing telemetry.log into ~/.gemini/extensions/test-extension. The installed extension depends on test-rig state, and telemetry written during the install can make the copy racy. The extension should live in a dedicated subdirectory (e.g. rig.mkdir('ext')) instead."
  },
  {
    "file": "integration-tests/test-helper.ts",
    "line": 309,
    "summary": "runCommand is a near copy-paste of run(): the spawn, stdout/stderr collection with KEEP_OUTPUT echo, and close/resolve/reject logic are all duplicated (about 45 lines).",
    "failure_scenario": "Any future fix to process handling (an error listener, timeouts, stdin handling, podman telemetry filtering) has to be made twice, and the two copies are already drifting apart (the stdin-closing behavior differs). A shared private spawnAndCollect(commandArgs, {stdin, stdinDoesNotEnd}) helper that both methods call would remove the copy."
  },
  {
    "file": "integration-tests/extensions-install.test.ts",
    "line": 26,
    "summary": "The test calls writeFileSync(join(rig.testDir!, ...)) directly instead of the existing rig.createFile helper, and it creates the TestRig at module level instead of inside the test like the other integration tests do.",
    "failure_scenario": "This duplicates TestRig.createFile, which already joins testDir and writes the file. Because the rig is shared at module scope, retry: 2 reuses the same rig across attempts and any second test added to the file would share its state. Also, the test title says 'verifies a command', but no extension command is ever checked."
  }
]
```
